"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have successfully constructed a configuration for \\(n=3\\) with exactly \\(k=1\\) sunny line. Hence such a configuration exists.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n- List the required points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le4\\).  \n- Choose two non\u2011sunny lines (one vertical, one with slope \\(-1\\)) that together cover five of the six points.  \n- The remaining uncovered point is then joined to a previously covered point by a line whose slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\), nor \\(-1\\); this line is sunny.  \n- Verify that all six points lie on at least one of the three lines and that exactly one line is sunny.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a,b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le 4\\}\n   =\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe must exhibit three distinct lines \\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\) such that  \n\n1. every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n2. exactly one of the lines is *sunny* (i.e. its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).\n\n---\n\n#### Step 1 \u2013 Choose two non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nTake  \n\n\\[\nL_{2}:\\;x=1 ,\\qquad L_{3}:\\;x+y=4 .\n\\]\n\nBoth are non\u2011sunny: \\(L_{2}\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, and \\(L_{3}\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nPoints of \\(S\\) on these lines:\n\n* On \\(L_{2}\\): \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\);\n* On \\(L_{3}\\): \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).\n\nThus after \\(L_{2}\\) and \\(L_{3}\\) are placed, the only point of \\(S\\) not yet covered is \\((2,1)\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Step 2 \u2013 Add a single sunny line covering the remaining point  \n\nSelect any point already covered and join it to \\((2,1)\\) with a line whose slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\).  \nChoose the point \\((1,3)\\). The line through \\((2,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{3-1}{1-2}= -2,\n\\]\n\nwhich is admissible. Its equation is  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:\\; y-1 = -2(x-2)\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad y = -2x+5 .\n\\]\n\nSince the slope \\(-2\\) is different from the prohibited values, \\(L_{1}\\) is a sunny line.\n\n---\n\n#### Step 3 \u2013 Verify coverage of all points  \n\n| Point \\((a,b)\\) | Lies on which line(s)? |\n|-----------------|------------------------|\n| \\((1,1)\\) | \\(L_{2}\\) |\n| \\((1,2)\\) | \\(L_{2}\\) |\n| \\((1,3)\\) | \\(L_{2}, L_{3}, L_{1}\\) |\n| \\((2,1)\\) | \\(L_{1}\\) |\n| \\((2,2)\\) | \\(L_{3}\\) |\n| \\((3,1)\\) | \\(L_{3}\\) |\n\nEvery point of \\(S\\) belongs to at least one of the three lines.\n\n---\n\n#### Step 4 \u2013 Count sunny lines  \n\n- \\(L_{2}\\) is vertical \\(\\Rightarrow\\) not sunny.  \n- \\(L_{3}\\) has slope \\(-1\\) \\(\\Rightarrow\\) not sunny.  \n- \\(L_{1}\\) has slope \\(-2\\) \\(\\Rightarrow\\) sunny.\n\nHence exactly one of the three lines is sunny, i.e. \\(k=1\\).\n\n---\n\n**Conclusion:**  \nFor \\(n=3\\) there exists a configuration of three distinct lines satisfying the covering condition with exactly one sunny line. An explicit example is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&:\\; y = -2x+5 \\quad (\\text{sunny}),\\\\\nL_{2}&:\\; x = 1 \\quad (\\text{non\u2011sunny}),\\\\\nL_{3}&:\\; x+y = 4 \\quad (\\text{non\u2011sunny}).\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]"
