s equality is\nsatisfied for the unique admissible \\(s\\).  In any case each point of\n\\(D_s\\) lies on the line \\(S_i\\) with the same first coordinate \\(i\\).  Thus\nevery point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to exactly one of the \\(n\\) sunny\nlines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Non‑existence for \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the\nrequirements and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, where \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Reduction to diagonal omissions\n\nAny non‑sunny line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line\n\\(x+y=0\\).  Replacing a horizontal or vertical line by the diagonal line\nthat contains the same subset of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) does not change the set of\ncovered points and does not affect the number of sunny lines.  Hence we may\nassume that every non‑sunny line used is a diagonal line \\(x+y=s\\).\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D=\\{\\,s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\mid x+y=s\\text{ is **not** used}\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause we have exactly \\(n\\) lines, the number of omitted diagonals equals\nthe number of sunny lines; therefore  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal D|=k. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Size of the omitted set\n\nFor a fixed sum \\(s\\) the set \\(D_s\\) contains \\(s-1\\) points.\nA sunny line can intersect the diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) in at most one point,\nhence covering all points of \\(D_s\\) requires at least \\(s-1\\) sunny lines.\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\ns-1\\le k\\qquad\\text{for every }s\\in\\mathcal D,\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\ni.e. \\(s\\le k+1\\).  Thus  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSince the right‑hand side of (4) contains exactly \\(k\\) elements and\n\\(|\\mathcal D|=k\\) by (2), we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D=