umber of points minus the maximal number that can be supplied by the non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   Using (2) and summing over all \\(s\\) we obtain a lower bound\n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}\\bigl[(s-1)-2\\bigr]\n   \\;=\\;\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-3)\n   \\;=\\;\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore the sunny lines must cover at least \\(\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2}\\) points.  \n   Combining this with (3) yields\n\n   \\[\n   k(n-1)\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2}\n   \\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n   k\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n-2}{2}.\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(k\\le n-1\\) by hypothesis, inequality (5) forces  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\Bigl\\{\\frac{n-2}{2},\\frac{n-1}{2},\\dots ,n-1\\Bigr\\}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(n\\ge 3\\) the only integer values satisfying (6) and the original bound \\(k\\le n-1\\) are \\(k=n-1\\) and, when \\(n\\) is even, also \\(k=\\frac{n}{2}\\).  \n   We now show that none of these possibilities can actually occur.\n\n   *If \\(k=n-1\\):* then there is exactly one non\u2011sunny line.  \n   That line can be either horizontal, vertical or diagonal.  \n   In each case it can cover at most \\(n\\) points (a horizontal or vertical line meets each diagonal in at most one point, a diagonal line can cover at most one whole diagonal, i.e. at most \\(n\\) points).  \n   The remaining \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-n = \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\) points would have to be covered by the single sunny line, which is impossible because a sunny line meets each diagonal in at most one point, giving at most \\(n-1\\) points.\n\n   *If \\(k=\\frac{n}{2}\\) (possible only when \\(n\\) is even):* then there are \\(n-\\frac{n}{2}= \\frac{n}{2}\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   Even if all of them were diagonal lines, they could cover at most \\(\\frac{n}{2}\\) whole diagonals, i.e. at most \\(\\frac{n}{2}\\cdot\\frac{n}{2}= \\frac{n^{2}}{4}\\) points.  \n   The remaining points number  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-\\frac{n^{2}}{4}\n   =\\frac{n(2n+2-n)}{4}\n   =\\frac{n(n+2)}{4},\n   \\]\n\n   which exceeds the maximal possible contribution of the \\(\\frac{n}{2}\\) sunny lines, namely \\(\\frac{n}{2}\\cdot (n-1)=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\), for every \\(n\\ge 4\\).  Hence this case is also impossible.\n\n   Consequently no configuration exists with \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n*The detailed proof of the constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) and the impossibility for \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\) is given in the summary above.  The existence of a configuration with \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny) remains an open sub\u2011problem within this write\u2011up.*"

