on\u2011sunny**.  \n   - \\(L_{3}\\) has slope \\(1\\); it is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, so \\(L_{3}\\) is **sunny**.\n\n   Consequently exactly one of the three lines (\\(L_{3}\\)) is sunny, i.e. \\(k=1\\).\n\n3. **Coverage of the required points.**  \n   Compute the points lying on each line:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_{1}:&\\;x+y=3 \\Longrightarrow (1,2),\\;(2,1);\\\\\n   L_{2}:&\\;x+y=4 \\Longrightarrow (1,3),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1);\\\\\n   L_{3}:&\\;y=x   \\Longrightarrow (1,1),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,3),\\dots\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   Hence\n   \\[\n   S\\subseteq L_{1}\\cup L_{2}\\cup L_{3},\n   \\]\n   because each element of \\(S\\) appears in at least one of the three lines:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c}\n   \\text{Point} & \\text{Line containing it}\\\\ \\hline\n   (1,1) & L_{3}\\\\\n   (1,2) & L_{1}\\\\\n   (1,3) & L_{2}\\\\\n   (2,1) & L_{1}\\\\\n   (2,2) & L_{2},\\,L_{3}\\\\\n   (3,1) & L_{2}\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\nAll conditions of the problem are satisfied. Therefore for \\(n=3\\) the integer \\(k=1\\) is realizable. \u220e"
