  \nThe slopes that are sunny are:\n\n* \\(m((1,1),(2,2)) = 1\\);\n* \\(m((1,2),(3,1)) = -\\dfrac12\\);\n* \\(m((1,3),(2,1)) = -2\\).\n\nAll other pairs give slopes \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), which are non\u2011sunny.  \nConsequently the only sunny lines that contain at least two points of \\(S\\) are\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&:\\; y = x, &&\\text{contains }(1,1),(2,2);\\\\[2mm]\nL_{2}&:\\; y = -\\frac12 x + \\frac52, &&\\text{contains }(1,2),(3,1);\\\\[2mm]\nL_{3}&:\\; y = -2x + 5, &&\\text{contains }(1,3),(2,1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEach of these three lines is sunny, and the two points it contains are unique to that line.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Selecting two sunny lines\n\nChoose any two of the sunny lines.  \nSuppose we select \\(L_{1}\\) and \\(L_{2}\\).  \nThen the points of \\(S\\) already covered are\n\n\\[\n\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\}\\cup\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\}\n   =\\{(1,1),(2,2),(1,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe two remaining points are \\((1,3)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  \nThese satisfy \\(x+y=4\\); hence they lie on the line\n\n\\[\nL_{4}:\\; x+y=4,\n\\]\n\nwhose slope is \\(-1\\).  Therefore \\(L_{4}\\) is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Verification of the configuration\n\nThe three lines\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{L_{1}: y = x},\\qquad\n\\boxed{L_{2}: y = -\\frac12 x + \\frac52},\\qquad\n\\boxed{L_{4}: x + y = 4}\n\\]\n\nare pairwise distinct (their slopes are \\(1\\), \\(-\\tfrac12\\), and \\(-1\\), respectively).  \n\nEvery point of \\(S\\) belongs to at least one of them:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{Point} & \\text{Containing line(s)}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1) & L_{1}\\\\\n(2,2) & L_{1}\\\\\n(1,2) & L_{2}\\\\\n(3,1) & L_{2}\\\\\n(1,3) & L_{4}\\\\\n(2,1) & L_{4}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nExactly two of the three lines (\\(L_{1}\\) and \\(L_{2}\\)) are sunny, while \\(L_{4}\\) is non\u2011sunny.\n\nThus the required configuration exists for \\(n=3\\) and \\(k=2\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{A configuration with }n=3,\\;k=2\\text{ exists, e.g. the three lines above.}}\n\\]"
