ere exist \\(t\\) distinct lines in the plane such that  \n> (i) every point of \\(\\mathcal{P}_t\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n> (ii) exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.”\n\nWe shall prove \\(\\mathcal{S}(t)\\) for all \\(t\\ge 3\\) by induction on \\(t\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Base of induction (\\(t=3\\))\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal{P}_3\\) consists of the three points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1).\n\\]\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the three diagonals \\(x+y=2,3,4\\) (all non‑sunny) cover the three points.  \n* \\(k=3\\): take three arbitrary sunny lines, each passing through exactly one of the three points (e.g. \\(y=2x\\) through \\((1,1)\\), \\(y=2x-1\\) through \\((1,2)\\), \\(y=2x-3\\) through \\((2,1)\\)).  \n* \\(k=1\\) and \\(k=2\\): explicit families are given below.\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\nk & \\text{family of three lines} \\\\ \\hline\n1 & \\{\\,x+y=2,\\;x=1,\\;y=1\\,\\} \\\\\n2 & \\{\\,x+y=2,\\;x=1,\\;y=2\\,\\}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nIn each case the required numbers of sunny lines are evident, and all three points are covered. Hence \\(\\mathcal{S}(3)\\) holds.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Inductive step\n\nAssume \\(\\mathcal{S}(t-1)\\) holds for some \\(t\\ge 4\\).  \nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le t\\).  \nSet  \n\n\\[\nm:=t-k\\qquad (0\\le m\\le t).\n\\]\n\n#### 4.1  The non‑sunny part\n\nTake the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,m).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(V_i\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.  \nThese lines cover precisely the points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_t\\) whose abscissa satisfies \\(a\\le m\\).\n\n#### 4.2  Reduction to a smaller instance\n\nConsider the subset of \\(\\mathcal{P}_t\\) not yet covered:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{R}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal{P}_t\\mid a\\ge m+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(a'=a-m\\) (so \\(a'\\ge 1\\)).  \nBecause \\(a+b\\le t+1\\),\n\n\\[\na'+b=(a-m)+