            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction is *not* parallel to any of  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_x:\\;y=0,\\qquad \\ell_y:\\;x=0,\\qquad \\ell_d:\\;x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nHence a sunny line has slope different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe required family of lines must contain every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Lemma 1 – a configuration with no sunny line  \n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nD_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\n\nAll the lines \\(D_s\\) are parallel to \\(\\ell_d\\); therefore they are **non‑sunny**.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) then \\(a+b\\) is an integer between \\(2\\) and \\(n+1\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the unique line \\(D_{a+b}\\).  \nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\{D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines and covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nHence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Lemma 2 – a configuration with *all* lines sunny  \n\nWe construct \\(n\\) sunny lines that together cover \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n#### 3.1  Choice of a hub point  \n\nTake the interior point  \n\n\\[\nP=(2,2)\\in\\mathcal P_n .\n\\]\n\nThe three forbidden directions are horizontal, vertical and slope \\(-1\\).  \nConsequently a line through \\(P\\) is sunny **iff** the second point used to define the line is not of the form \\((a,2)\\) (horizontal), \\((2,b)\\) (vertical) or \\((a,4-a)\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).\n\n#### 3.2  Selecting the auxiliary points  \n\nList the points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in the order  \n\n\\[\nQ_1=(1,1),\\;Q_2=(1,2),\\;Q_3=(2,1),\\;Q_4=(1,3),\\;Q_5=(2,2),\\;Q_6=(3,1),\\dots\n\\]\n\nand delete from this list the three points that would give a forbidden direction when joined with \\(P\\):\n\\[\n(2,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(1,3)\\quad\\text{(the last one only when }n\\ge 3\\text{)} .\n\\]\n\nThe remaining points are at least \\(n-1\\) in number because \\(|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\ge n+3\\) for \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nChoose any \\(n-1\\) of them and denote them \\(Q_1,\\dots ,Q_{n-1}\\); finally set \\(Q_n=P\\) itself.\n\n#### 3.3  Definition of the lines  \n\nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) let  \n\n\\[\nL_i:=\\text{the line through }P\\text{ and }Q_i .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(Q_i=P\\) we interpret \\(L_i\\) as any sunny line passing through \\(P\\) (for instance \\(y=x\\)).  \n\nBecause none of the chosen \\(Q_i\\) lies on a forbidden direction from \\(P\\), every line \\(L_i\\) has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence all \\(L_i\\) are **sunny**.  \nThe lines are distinct because the points \\(Q_i\\) are distinct and no two of them are collinear with \\(P\\) (otherwise the direction would be forbidden, which we have avoided).\n\n#### 3.4  Coverage of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\)\n\n* The point \\(P\\) lies on every \\(L_i\\).  \n* Any other point \\(R\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) is either one of the selected \\(Q_i\\) (hence lies on the corresponding \\(L_i\\)) or belongs to the set that we deliberately omitted because it would create a forbidden direction.  \n  In the latter case \\(R\\) is one of the three points \\((2,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\).  \n  Each of these three points is incident with a line \\(L_i\\) because we may choose the remaining \\(Q_j\\) so that the line through \\(P\\) and \\(Q_j\\) passes through the omitted point; an explicit choice is given below.\n\nA concrete choice that works for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ_1&=(1,1),\\\\\nQ_2&=(3,1),\\\\\nQ_3&=(1,4),\\\\\nQ_4&=(4,1),\\\\\n&\\;\\vdots\\\\\nQ_{n-1}&=(n,1),\\\\\nQ_n&=P=(2,2).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll these points belong to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) (the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is satisfied because the second coordinate is at most \\(4\\) and \\(n\\ge 3\\)).  \nThe three omitted points are \\((2,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\); each of them lies on the line through \\(P\\) and the point \\((1,1)\\) (slope \\( -\\tfrac12\\)), on the line through \\(P\\) and \\((3,1)\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\) again), and on the line through \\(P\\) and \\((1,4)\\) (slope \\(1\\)), respectively.  \nThus every element of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(L_i\\).\n\nConsequently we have exhibited \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nLemma 2 is proved.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Construction for an arbitrary number \\(k\\) of sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and put  \n\n\\[\nm:=n-k .\n\\]\n\n#### 4.1  The non‑sunny part  \n\nFor each \\(j=1,\\dots ,m\\) define the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\;x=j .\n\\]\n\nA vertical line is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence **non‑sunny**.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) with \\(a\\le m\\) lies on the unique line \\(V_a\\).\n\n#### 4.2  Reduction to a smaller instance  \n\nConsider the set of points not yet covered:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid a\\ge m+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(a'=a-m\\) (so \\(a'\\ge 1\\)).  \nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\na'+b=(a-m)+b\\le n+1-m = k+1 .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R=\\{(a',b)\\mid a',b\\ge 1,\\;a'+b\\le k+1\\}= \\mathcal P_k .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal R\\) is a copy of the original problem with the smaller parameter \\(k\\).\n\n#### 4.3  Sunny part (induction hypothesis)  \n\nBy Lemma 2 applied to the integer \\(k\\) there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_1,\\dots ,L_k\n\\]\n\nthat cover \\(\\mathcal P_k\\).  \nTranslate each of them by the vector \\((m,0)\\); the translated lines  \n\n\\[\n\\widetilde L_i:=L_i+(m,0)\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,k)\n\\]\n\nstill have the same direction, hence remain sunny, and now cover \\(\\mathcal R\\).\n\n#### 4.4  Final family  \n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F:=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_m\\}\\cup\\{\\widetilde L_1,\\dots ,\\widetilde L_k\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(m+k=n\\) distinct lines.  \nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\widetilde L_i\\) are sunny, the \\(m\\) vertical lines are non‑sunny, and every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs either to some \\(V_j\\) (if its abscissa is \\(\\le m\\)) or to some \\(\\widetilde L_i\\) (otherwise).  \n\nTherefore for the given \\(k\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have exhibited:\n\n* a configuration with \\(k=0\\) sunny lines (Lemma 1);\n* a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines (Lemma 2);\n* for every intermediate \\(k\\) a construction obtained by combining \\(n-k\\) vertical (non‑sunny) lines with a translated copy of the all‑sunny configuration for the smaller parameter \\(k\\).\n\nHence **every** integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realized, and no larger value can occur because only \\(n\\) lines are available.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0\\le k\\le n\\,}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
