actly \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines and covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nHence the value  \n\n\\[\nk=0\n\\]\n\nis always attainable.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Reduction to the “all‑sunny’’ sub‑problem  \n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and set  \n\n\\[\nm:=n-k .\n\\]\n\n#### 3.1  Non‑sunny vertical lines  \n\nFor each \\(j=1,\\dots ,m\\) define  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\;x=j .\n\\]\n\nA vertical line is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore it is **non‑sunny**.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) with \\(a\\le m\\) lies on the unique line \\(V_a\\).\n\n#### 3.2  The remaining region  \n\nThe points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that are not covered by the vertical lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid a\\ge m+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(a'=a-m\\) (so \\(a'\\ge1\\)) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R=\\{(a',b)\\mid a',b\\ge1,\\;a'+b\\le k+1\\}\n          =\\mathcal P_k .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal R\\) is exactly the triangular set \\(\\mathcal P_k\\) associated with the smaller integer \\(k\\).\n\n#### 3.3  Sunny covering of \\(\\mathcal P_k\\) (assumed)  \n\nAssume that for the integer \\(k\\) there exists a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_k=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\n\\]\n\nof **exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines** such that every point of \\(\\mathcal P_k\\) lies on at least one of the \\(L_i\\).\n\nTranslate each line by the vector \\((m,0)\\):\n\n\\[\n\\widetilde L_i:=L_i+(m,0)\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,k).\n\\]\n\nTranslation does not change the direction of a line, so each \\(\\widetilde L_i\\) is still sunny.  \nBecause the translation adds \\(m\\) to the \\(x\\)-coordinate, the set \\(\\{\\widetilde L_i\\}\\) covers precisely the set \\(\\mathcal R\\).\n\n#### 3.4  Final family  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F:=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_m\\}\\cup\\{\\widetilde L_1,\\dots ,\\widetilde L_k\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal F\\) consists of \\(m+k=n\\) distinct lines.  \nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\widetilde L_i\\) are sunny, while the \\(m\\) vertical lines are non‑sunny.  \nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs either to some \\(V_j\\) (if its abscissa is \\(\\le m\\)) or to some \\(\\widetilde L_i\\) (otherwise). Hence \\(\\mathcal F\\) satisfies both requirements of the problem.\n\nConsequently, **the original problem is reduced to the existence of an “all‑sunny’’ configuration for every positive integer \\(k\\)**.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Open sub‑problem  \n\nThe construction claimed in the original answer for the all‑sunny case (Lemma 2) was incorrect: some of the lines had forbidden slopes (horizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\)), and the argument that every point of \\(\\mathcal P_k\\) lies on one of the chosen lines was incomplete.  \n\nA correct, gap‑free construction of \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_k\\) for an arbitrary \\(k\\ge1\\) is therefore required to complete the classification.  \n\n*At present this sub‑problem remains unsolved within the present answer.*"
