            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n*The detailed solution below contains only the parts that have been proved rigorously; the construction of an all‑sunny family for an arbitrary positive integer \\(k\\) is left as an open sub‑problem.*\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Notation  \n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its direction is not parallel to any of the three lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_x:\\;y=0,\\qquad \\ell_y:\\;x=0,\\qquad \\ell_d:\\;x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nEquivalently, a line is sunny iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Lemma 1 – configuration with no sunny line  \n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) let  \n\n\\[\nD_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\n\nAll the lines \\(D_s\\) are parallel to \\(\\ell_d\\); therefore they are **non‑sunny**.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) then \\(a+b\\) is an integer between \\(2\\) and \\(n+1\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the unique line \\(D_{a+b}\\).  \n\nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\{D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines and covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nHence the value  \n\n\\[\nk=0\n\\]\n\nis always attainable.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Reduction to the “all‑s