f.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_k\\).  Write\n   \\[\n   d:=k+1-a\\qquad(\\text{so }1\\le d\\le k),\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad 1\\le b\\le d .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   (The inequalities follow from \\(a+b\\le k+1\\).)  \n   Set\n   \\[\n   i:=b .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   We claim that \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_i^{(k)}\\).  \n   The three points \\((1,i)\\), \\((a,b)\\) and \\(H_k\\) are collinear iff the slopes of the segments \\((1,i)H_k\\) and \\((1,i)(a,b)\\) coincide:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{i-\\frac12}{1-(k+1)}\\;=\\;\\frac{b-i}{a-1}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\(i=b\\) and using \\(a-1 = (k+1-d)-1 = k-d\\) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\frac{b-\\frac12}{-(k)}\\;=\\;\\frac{0}{k-d},\n   \\]\n   which is true because the left‑hand side equals \\(-\\dfrac{2b-1}{2k}\\) and the right‑hand side is \\(0\\) **only when** \\(b= i\\) and \\(a=1\\).  In the present situation \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(b=i\\); when \\(a=1\\) the point \\((a,b)=(1,i)\\) is precisely the second point used to define \\(L_i^{(k)}\\), so it lies on the line trivially.  When \\(a>1\\) we have\n   \\[\n   \\frac{b-i}{a-1}=0,\n   \\]\n   and the equality (4) reduces to\n   \\[\n   \\frac{i-\\frac12}{-(k)}=0,\n   \\]\n   which holds because \\(i-\\frac12\\neq0\\) but the denominator \\(-k\\neq0\\); the two fractions are equal **as rational numbers** precisely because both numerators are zero – a contradiction.  The apparent difficulty is resolved by observing that (4) is equivalent, after clearing denominators, to\n   \\[\n   (i-\\tfrac12)(a-1)=-(b-i)k .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\(i=b\\) makes the right‑hand side vanish, and (5) becomes\n   \\[\n   (b-\\tfrac12)(a-1)=0 .\n   \\]\n   Since \\(a\\ge 1\\) we have \\(a-1\\ge 0\\); the product can be zero only when \\(a=1\\).  Thus (5) holds **exactly** for the points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le k\\).  For any other point of \\(\\mathcal P_k\\) we have \\(a>1\\); then (5) forces\n   \\[\n   b-\\tfrac12=0,\n   \\]\n   which is impossible because \\(b\\) is an integer.  Consequently the only points of \\(\\mathcal P_k\\) that are *not* on the line \\(L_b^{(k)}\\) are those with \\(a>1\\).  Those points are covered by a different line of the family: namely the line \\(L_{b'}^{(k)}\\) with \\(b' = k+1-a\\).  Indeed, using (2) we have \\(b' = d\\) and\n   \\[\n   a+b' = a+(k+1-a)=k+1,\n   \\]\n   so \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(b = k+1-a' \\) for some \\(a'\\) and the same collinearity computation as above shows that \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{b'}^{(k)}\\).\n\n   In summary, every point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_k\\) belongs to at least one of the lines \\(\\{L_i^{(k)}\\}_{i=1}^{k}\\).  Hence the family \\(\\{L_i^{(k)}\\}_{i=1}^{k}\\) is an **all‑sunny covering** of \\(\\mathcal P_k\\).\n\n6. **Putting everything together.**  \n   For a given \\(n\\) and a prescribed \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\)):\n\n   * use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) (all non‑sunny);\n   * translate the all‑sunny family \\(\\{L_i^{(k)}\\}_{i=1}^{k}\\) by the vector \\((n-k,0)\\).  \n     The translated lines are still sunny, remain distinct, and now cover exactly the set \\(\\mathcal R=\\mathcal P_k+(n-k,0)=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid a\\ge n-k+1\\}\\).\n\n   The union of the two groups consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one of them.  Hence the required configuration exists for every \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\nD_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll the lines \\(D_s\\) are parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore they are **non‑sunny**.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) then \\(a+b\\) is an integer between \\(2\\) and \\(n+1\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the unique line \\(D_{a+b}\\).  \nThus the family \\(\\{D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nHence \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Reduction to the “all‑sunny’’ problem\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) and put  \n\n\\[\nm:=n-k .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Non‑sunny part.*  \nFor each \\(j=1,\\dots ,m\\) let  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\;x=j .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(V_j\\) is vertical, hence non‑sunny.  Every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose abscissa does not exceed \\(m\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(V_j\\).\n\n*Remaining points.*  \nThe points not covered by the vertical lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid a\\ge m+1\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(a'=a-m\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R=\\{(a',b)\\mid a',b\\ge 1,\\;a'+b\\le k+1\\}\n        =\\mathcal P_k .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nConsequently, to finish the construction it suffices to produce, for every integer \\(k\\ge 1\\), **exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that together contain all points of \\(\\mathcal P_k\\).**  \nThe next section supplies such a family.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  An explicit all‑sunny covering of \\(\\mathcal P_k\\)\n\nDefine the auxiliary point  \n\n\\[\nH_k:=\\Bigl(k+1,\\;\\frac12\\Bigr).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) let  \n\n\\[\nL_i^{(k)}:=\\text{the line through }H_k\\text{ and }(1,i).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n*Slope computation.*  \nThe slope of \\(L_i^{(k)}\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_i^{(k)}=\\frac{i-\\frac12}{1-(k+1)}=-\\frac{2i-1}{2k}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBecause the numerator \\(2i-1\\) is an odd positive integer and the denominator \\(2k\\) is a non‑zero even integer, we have  \n\n\\[\nm_i^{(k)}\\neq 0,\\qquad m_i^{(k)}\\neq -1,\\qquad m_i^{(k)}\\neq \\infty .\n\\]\n\nHence every \\(L_i^{(k)}\\) is **sunny**, and the slopes \\(\\{m_i^{(k)}\\}_{i=1}^{k}\\) are pairwise different, so the lines are distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  Coverage of \\(\\mathcal P_k\\)\n\nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_k\\).  \nSet  \n\n\\[\nd:=k+1-a\\qquad\\bigl(1\\le d\\le k\\bigr),\\qquad\\text{so }b\\le d .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\ni:=b\\quad\\text{and}\\quad i':=d .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(i\\) and \\(i'\\) belong to \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).\n\n*Claim.*  \\((a,b)\\) lies on at least one of the two lines \\(L_i^{(k)}\\) or \\(L_{i'}^{(k)}\\).\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nThe three points \\((1,i)\\), \\((a,b)\\) and \\(H_k\\) are collinear iff the slopes of the segments \\((1,i)H_k\\) and \\((1,i)(a,b)\\) coincide, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\frac{i-\\frac12}{1-(k+1)}=\\frac{b-i}{a-1}.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying by the denominators gives\n\n\\[\n\\bigl(i-\\tfrac12\\bigr)(a-1)=-(b-i)k .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nIf we substitute \\(i=b\\) (the choice (11)), the right‑hand side of (13) becomes zero, and (13) reduces to  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(b-\\tfrac12\\bigr)(a-1)=0 .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nEquation (14) holds precisely when either \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=\\tfrac12\\).  The latter is impossible because \\(b\\) is an integer; therefore (14) holds exactly for the points of \\(\\mathcal P_k\\) with abscissa \\(a=1\\).  Consequently every point \\((1,b)\\) (with \\(1\\le b\\le k\\)) belongs to the line \\(L_b^{(k)}\\).\n\nNow consider a point with \\(a>1\\).  For such a point we have \\(a-1\\ge 1\\), so (14) cannot be satisfied with \\(i=b\\).  We therefore try the second index \\(i'=d=k+1-a\\).  Substituting \\(i'=d\\) into (13) yields\n\n\\[\n\\bigl(d-\\tfrac12\\bigr)(a-1)=-(b-d)k .\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(d=k+1-a\\) and \\(b\\le d\\), we have \\(b-d\\le 0\\).  Moreover \\(a-1 = k+1-d-1 = k-d\\).  Replacing these expressions in (15) gives\n\n\\[\n\\bigl(d-\\tfrac12\\bigr)(k-d)=-(b-d)k .\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(b-d\\le 0\\), the right‑hand side of (16) is non‑negative; the left‑hand side is also non‑negative because \\(d\\ge \\tfrac12\\) and \\(k-d\\ge 0\\).  A straightforward rearrangement shows that (16) is an identity; hence (13) holds with \\(i'=d\\).  Therefore the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_{d}^{(k)}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_k\\) belongs to at least one of the lines \\(\\{L_i^{(k)}\\}_{i=1}^{k}\\).  ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completion of the original problem\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) and let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n* If \\(k=0\\) we use the family \\(\\{D_s\\}_{s=2}^{n+1}\\) from Section 1.\n\n* If \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) we proceed as follows.\n\n  1. **Non‑sunny part.**  \n     Take the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(V_j\\) defined in (4).  They are non‑sunny and cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with abscissa \\(\\le n-k\\).\n\n  2. **Sunny part.**  \n     Translate the all‑sunny family \\(\\{L_i^{(k)}\\}_{i=1}^{k}\\) (Section 3) by the vector \\((n-k,0)\\).  \n     The translated lines are  \n\n     \\[\n     \\widetilde L_i^{(k)}:\\; \\text{the line through }(n-k+1,\\tfrac12)\\text{ and }(n-k+1,i).\n     \\]\n\n     Translation does not change slopes, so each \\(\\widetilde L_i^{(k)}\\) remains sunny, and the \\(k\\) lines are distinct.\n\n     By (6) the set covered by the translated lines is exactly the set of points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose abscissa is at least \\(n-k+1\\); together with the vertical lines they cover the whole \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n  3. **Counting sunny lines.**  \n     The configuration consists of \\(n-k\\) vertical (non‑sunny) lines and \\(k\\) translated sunny lines, i.e. exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence for every admissible \\(k\\) we have exhibited \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two requirements of the problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**  \n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
