e parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore they are **non‑sunny**.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) then \\(a+b\\) is an integer between \\(2\\) and \\(n+1\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the unique line \\(D_{a+b}\\).  \nThus the family \\(\\{D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nHence \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Reduction to the “all‑sunny’’ problem\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) and put  \n\n\\[\nm:=n-k .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Non‑sunny part.*  \nFor each \\(j=1,\\dots ,m\\) let  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\;x=j .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(V_j\\) is vertical, hence non‑sunny.  Every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose abscissa does not exceed \\(m\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(V_j\\).\n\n*Remaining points.*  \nThe points not covered by the vertical lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid a\\ge m+1\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(a'=a-m\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R=\\{(a',b)\\mid a',b\\ge 1,\\;a'+b\\le k+1\\}\n        =\\mathcal P_k .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nConsequently, to finish the construction it suffices to produce, for every integer \\(k\\ge 1\\), **exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that together contain all points of \\(\\mathcal P_k\\).**  \nThe next section supplies such a family.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  An explicit all‑sunny covering of \\(\\mathcal P_k\\)\n\nDefine the auxiliary point  \n\n\\[\nH_k:=\\Bigl(k+1,\\;\\frac12\\Bigr).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) let  \n\n\\[\nL_i^{(k)}:=\\text{the line through }H_k\\text{ and }(1,i).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n*Slope computation.*  \nThe slope of \\(L_i^{(k)}\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_i^{(k)}=\\frac{i-\\frac12}{1-(k+1)}=-\\frac{2i-1}{2k}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBecause the numerator \\(2i-1\\) is an odd positive integer and the deno