------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\nD_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAll the lines \\(D_s\\) are parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore they are **non\u2011sunny**.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) then \\(a+b\\) is an integer between \\(2\\) and \\(n+1\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the unique line \\(D_{a+b}\\).  \nThus the \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines \\(\\{D_2,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}\\) satisfy the required covering condition.  \nHence \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Reduction for a general \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) and put  \n\n\\[\nm:=n-k .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Non\u2011sunny part.*  \nTake the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\;x=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,m).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(V_j\\) is vertical, therefore non\u2011sunny.  Every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose abscissa does not exceed \\(m\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(V_j\\).\n\n*Remaining points.*  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid a\\ge m+1\\}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(a'=a-m\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R=\\{(a',b)\\mid a',b\\ge 1,\\;a'+b\\le k+1\\}\n        =\\mathcal P_k .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus, after translating the whole picture by the vector \\((m,0)\\), the uncovered points are exactly the set \\(\\mathcal P_k\\).\n\nConsequently, the original problem for the pair \\((n,k)\\) is solvable **iff** we can find \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that together contain every point of \\(\\mathcal P_k\\)